                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "28233945",
            "user": "Seymour Edits",
            "timestamp": "21:05, 10 June 2020",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(12,268 bytes)",
            "change": 521,
            "summary": "(Targeted edits to incorporate key words)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "A split system air conditioner is a great option for keeping your home cool and comfortable in the summer months. These units are also quieter, easier to install, and more energy efficient than central air conditioning. This type of A/C is ductless, so all you have to do is install the cooling unit inside and the compressor and condenser unit outside, then run piping and a power cable between the units. If you don’t want to hire a professional to install a split system air conditioner and you have some experience with plumbing and electrical work, you can install the unit on your own. Each air conditioning unit is unique to its manufacturer, but the general installation process is the same.",
                    "#*Avoid locations where gas may leak or where oil mist or sulfur exists.",
                    "#*Most of these units have remote controls so that you can easily turn them on or off and adjust the temperature even if they’re mounted high on the wall.",
                    "*Check your outdoor condenser for debris and replace your air conditioner filter regularly—at least every 3 months.<ref>[v161403_b01]. 1 April 2020.</ref>",
                    "*If you want a split unit system that can both cool and heat your home, get a split system heat pump, or mini-split. These units act as both air conditioners and heaters.<ref>https://www.energy.gov/energysaver/heat-pump-systems/ductless-mini-split-heat-pumps</ref>"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "A split system air conditioner is a great option for keeping your home cool and comfortable in the summer months. This type of A/C is ductless, so all you have to do is install the cooling unit inside and the compressor and condenser unit outside, then run piping and a power cable between the units. If you don’t want to hire a professional to install a split system air conditioner and you have some experience with plumbing and electrical work, you can install the unit on your own. Each air conditioning unit is unique to its manufacturer, but the general installation process is the same.",
                    "",
                    "#*Avoid locations where gas may leak or where oil mist or sulfur exists.",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "*Check your outdoor condenser for debris and replace your air conditioner filter regularly—at least every 3 months.<ref>[v161403_b01]. 1 April 2020.</ref>",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    ""
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "28214806",
            "user": "WikiHow Expert Review",
            "timestamp": "19:31, 5 June 2020",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(11,747 bytes)",
            "change": 465,
            "summary": "(Integrated tips from NCA)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "*Since a split system AC is ductless, it's less susceptible to air leaks, which means it's often a more energy-efficient solution.<ref>[v161403_b01]. 1 April 2020.</ref>",
                    "*Make sure to choose the proper size air conditioner for your home to ensure it's energy efficient.<ref>[v161403_b01]. 1 April 2020.</ref>",
                    "*Check your outdoor condenser for debris and replace your air conditioner filter regularly—at least every 3 months.<ref>[v161403_b01]. 1 April 2020.</ref>"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    ""
                ]
            }
